{"assumptions":["(X_n) independent","c > 0 constant"],"created_at":"2026-10-07T19:28:52+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1d14ec11a3377e7a5e8111361b41381d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"Let (X_n)_{n=1}^\infty be independent real random variables, and fix c > 0. Define the truncated variables Y_n = X_n \mathbf{1}_{\{|X_n| \le c\}}. Then the series \sum_{n=1}^\infty X_n converges almost surely if and only if all three of the following series converge: (1) \sum_{n=1}^\infty \mathbb{P}(|X_n| > c) < \infty; (2) \sum_{n=1}^\infty \mathbb{E}[Y_n] \text{ converges in } \mathbb{R}; (3) \sum_{n=1}^\infty \text{Var}(Y_n) < \infty.

Preserved duplicate statement and commentary:
Let X_1, X_2, ... be independent random variables and fix a constant c > 0. For each i define the truncation Y_i = X_i 1(|X_i| <= c). Then the series sum_{i=1}^infinity X_i converges almost surely if and only if all three of the following series converge: sum_i P(|X_i| > c), sum_i E[Y_i], and sum_i Var(Y_i). If one of the three series diverges, the partial sums of sum X_i do not converge almost surely. [Durrett, Probability: Theory and Examples]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-09-07T12:18:10.806272+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1f5cb3bbe98637473036c4f1c3fa8c48c9a9bc64f0390b7a20a9c3fc688d4199","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 3 of arXiv:2512.19517, 'Spiking and Resetting'] For any $\sigma \ge 0$, and $0<a<b\le1$, let us define the analytic function
\begin{equation}

\begin{split}
Z(\cdot, \sigma : a,b): z \in D_{\sigma} (a,b) \mapsto Z(z,\sigma : a,b ):=\frac{1}{\sigma + f(0) + (1-z) f(0) (1/a -1/b)} \in \mathbb C 
\end{split}
\end{equation}
on the open disc $D_\sigma (a,b)$ of radius $R_\sigma (a,b)$, where
\begin{equation}

\begin{split}
R_\sigma (a,b) = 1+\cfrac{\sigma + f(0)}{f(0) (1/a -1/b)} >1 \ .
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Then for any $z\in D_{\sigma} (a,b)$ we have 
 \begin{equation}

\begin{split}
\lim_{\varepsilon \to 0} Z^\varepsilon (z,\sigma : a,b) = Z(z,\sigma : a,b ) \ .
\end{split}
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":["H a Hilbert space (complete); K : H → H compact linear","the statements concern λI − K with λ ≠ 0, normalised to λ = 1"],"created_at":"2026-09-13T01:59:57.492717+00:00","domain":"functional analysis, Riesz–Schauder theory, compact operators","id":"claim_22a9aef86baaa158148e612db4904e9458450f7a225443cc98c30dec7fad3de3","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"Let H be a Hilbert space (real or complex; the Banach-space version is identical with K* the Banach adjoint acting on the dual) and let K : H → H be a compact linear operator. Put T = I − K; the Hilbert adjoint is T* = I − K*, also identity minus compact. Then:
(a) ker T is finite-dimensional;
(b) range T is closed, range T = (ker T*)^⊥ and range T* = (ker T)^⊥;
(c) ker T = {0} if and only if range T = H (injective ⇔ surjective ⇔ bijective with bounded inverse);
(d) dim ker T = dim ker T* = codim range T < ∞, i.e. T is a Fredholm operator of index zero.

The alternative. EITHER the homogeneous equation u − Ku = 0 has only the trivial solution, and then u − Ku = f has exactly one solution for every f ∈ H, depending continuously on f; OR u − Ku = 0 has a d-dimensional space of solutions with 1 ≤ d < ∞, and then u − Ku = f is solvable if and only if ⟨f, w⟩ = 0 for every solution w of the adjoint homogeneous equation w − K*w = 0 (whose solution space is also d-dimensional); in that case the solution set is u_0 + ker T, an affine space of dimension d.

Spectral consequences. For each λ ≠ 0 apply the above to K/λ: σ(K) \ {0} consists of eigenvalues of finite multiplicity, is finite or a sequence tending to 0, and for such λ the generalised eigenspace ∪_m ker(λI − K)^m is finite-dimensional. If dim H = ∞ then 0 ∈ σ(K).

Where it fails. Compactness is essential: for the unilateral shift S on ℓ^2 (bounded, not compact) I − S is injective but not surjective (1 ∈ σ(S), the closed unit disc), so (c) fails. The alternative concerns I − K, not K itself: nothing is claimed at λ = 0; a compact K on an infinite-dimensional H is never surjective yet may be injective (diag(1/n) on ℓ^2), so 'K injective ⇒ K invertible' is false. In the second alternative the solvability condition is orthogonality to ker(I − K*), not to ker(I − K); they coincide when K is self-adjoint (or normal) but not in general. On an incomplete inner-product space closedness of the range and (c) can fail.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-09-07T12:18:08.278931+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_27e525400541a2c7cfb2664d66bf89dfe55f5347c5d93f4887c35d01a8ab4378","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2505.20799, 'A note on the improved sparse Hanson-Wright inequalities'] Assume that $m, n, k\in\mathbb{N}$ satisfies $k\le \min\{m, n\}$. Let $0<p\le 1$ and $\varepsilon>0$. Then for every matrix $X\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}$ of rank $k$ and any constant $r$ such that $$k\ge r\ge C_1\log \frac{mn}{\eta}\cdot\max\{ \frac{ p}{\varepsilon^2}, \frac{1 }{\varepsilon} \},$$ then Algorithm~\ref{alg:low_rank} outputs a matrix $Y\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}$ of rank no more than $r$ such that with probability at least $1-\eta$,
    \begin{align}
        \Vert X-Y\Vert_{\infty}\le \frac{k\varepsilon}{p\sqrt{mn}}\sqrt{\mu_\mathrm{col}\mu_\mathrm{row}}\cdot\Vert X\Vert_{l_2\to l_2},
    \end{align}
    where $\mu_\mathrm{col}$ and $\mu_\mathrm{row}$ are the coherence of the column and row spaces of $X$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":["X = R^2 minus one point, identified with C*; loops are continuous maps of [0,1] with equal endpoints","homotopies are required to take values in X throughout (they may not cross the puncture)","the winding number is defined via a continuous lift through the covering map t -> e^{2 pi i t}; no differentiability is assumed","the contour-integral formula additionally assumes the loop is piecewise C^1 (or rectifiable)","the classification is asserted for exactly one puncture in the plane, not for several punctures or for higher dimensions"],"created_at":"2026-09-12T19:09:43.732811+00:00","domain":"algebraic topology, fundamental group, winding number","id":"claim_28bd28eedced31c0ae01722cabf8da2248d1e692201e01776dffd1fa92bf977c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"Let X = R^2 minus the origin, identified with the nonzero complex numbers C*, and fix a base point x_0. For a CONTINUOUS loop gamma : [0,1] -> X with gamma(0) = gamma(1), write gamma(t) = |gamma(t)| e^{2 pi i theta(t)} with theta : [0,1] -> R continuous (such a lift exists and is unique once theta(0) is chosen, by the path lifting property of the covering map t -> e^{2 pi i t} from R onto the unit circle). The WINDING NUMBER is n(gamma) = theta(1) - theta(0), an integer independent of the choice of lift.

Then gamma -> n(gamma) induces a group ISOMORPHISM from pi_1(X, x_0) onto the integers Z. Consequently:
 (i) two loops at x_0 are homotopic rel endpoints in X if and only if they have the same winding number;
 (ii) since Z is abelian, conjugacy classes are singletons, so two loops (not necessarily based at the same point) are FREELY homotopic in X if and only if they have the same winding number; free homotopy classes of loops correspond bijectively to Z;
 (iii) a loop is null-homotopic in X exactly when its winding number is 0;
 (iv) the winding number is additive under concatenation and changes sign under reversal;
 (v) for a piecewise C^1 loop, n(gamma) = (1 / (2 pi i)) * (contour integral of dz/z along gamma), and for a loop avoiding an arbitrary point p the same statements hold for R^2 minus {p} with dz/(z - p).

SHARP EDGES. (1) Only CONTINUITY is needed; smoothness or rectifiability is required only for the integral formula in (v). (2) The homotopy must stay inside X at all times: a homotopy that is allowed to cross the puncture trivializes everything, which is precisely why the invariant exists. (3) The result is special to ONE puncture in dimension two. For R^n minus a point with n >= 3 the space is simply connected, every loop is null-homotopic and there is no winding number at all. (4) For TWO or more punctures the classification FAILS in this form: pi_1 of the plane minus two points is the free group on two generators, which is non-abelian, and the pair of winding numbers is only the image in the abelianization H_1 = Z x Z. The commutator loop a b a^{-1} b^{-1} has winding number 0 about both punctures yet is not null-homotopic. (5) Winding number is a homotopy invariant of the loop in X, not of its image set: two loops with the same image can have different winding numbers (traverse the circle once or twice).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-09-07T12:18:08.279088+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2b8ca9da5796df078534339d8c0121dac0032ee4773170358c77cbbb674505de","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 7 of arXiv:2505.20799, 'A note on the improved sparse Hanson-Wright inequalities'] Let $\xi$ be a random variable such that for $r\ge r_{0}$
	\begin{align}
		\Vert \xi\Vert_{L_r}\le \sum_{k=1}^{m}C_{k}r^{\beta_{k}}+C_{m+1},\nonumber
	\end{align}
	where $C_{1},\cdots, C_{m+1}> 0$ and $\beta_{1},\cdots, \beta_{m}>0$. Then for $t>0$,
	\begin{align}
		\mathbb{P}\big\{ \vert \xi\vert>e(mt+C_{m+1}) \big\}\le e^{r_{0}}\exp\Big(-\min\big\{\big(\frac{t}{C_{1}}\big)^{1/\beta_{1}},\cdots, \big(\frac{t}{C_{m}}\big)^{1/\beta_{m}}\big\}\Big).\nonumber
	\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":["Rare events have positive probability","Polish state space for weak-convergence criterion","No assertion that the toy process is percolation"],"created_at":"2026-10-10T08:06:45.814101+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_358a09f18cc575b995f83aeac9ef59b9d258fa8f4b9da339f3bfd50dc8c5cd1c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"AUTHOR-CHECKED; independent w346 audit PENDING. Unconditional convergence, even almost sure convergence, and an exact
survival probability of order 1/n do not suffice for a conditioned scaling limit.
For U uniform on (0,1), let A_n={U<=1/n} and Z_n=a_n*1_{A_n}, with a_n=1
for even n and 2 for odd n. Then Z_n->0 almost surely and n*P(A_n)=1 exactly,
but Law(Z_n|A_n) alternates between point masses at one and two.
A valid sufficient replacement on a Polish state space is: n*P(A_n)->c>0
and n*E[f(Z_n)*1_{A_n}]->c*integral f dnu for every bounded continuous f;
then Law(Z_n|A_n) converges weakly to nu.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-09T06:55:03+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_369a863ea0fe85dc19d6f9c3d7ff4d021b4f6648898d10db1b2f307969b473ae","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 5 of arXiv:2512.19517, 'Spiking and Resetting'] For any $t>0$ and any $0<a\le b\le1$, the probability distribution $P^\varepsilon_c( \cdot \, : \, t,a,b)$ over $\mathbb N_0$ converges in law to a Poisson distribution with parameter 
\begin{equation}

\begin{split}
f(0) \, t\,  \lambda_* ( [a,b]) = f(0)\,  t \, \int_a^b \cfrac{dx}{x^2} = f(0) \, t \,  (a^{-1}- b^{-1}) \  . 
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Furthermore, we have that, for any $n\ge 0$, the function
\begin{equation}

\begin{split}
\Phi_n^\varepsilon: r \in[0,\infty) \mapsto \mathbb P ( e_1^\varepsilon \ge r, N^\varepsilon_{(0,r]} ([a,b]) = n) \in [0,1]
\end{split}
\end{equation}
converges uniformly on any compact interval $[0,t]$, $t>0$, as $\varepsilon$ vanishes to 
\begin{equation}

\begin{split}
\Phi_n: r \in [0,\infty) \mapsto e^{-r f(0)} e^{-r \lambda_* ([a,b]} \frac{(r \lambda_* ([a,b]))^n}{n!} \in [0,1] \ .
\end{split}
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-09-07T12:18:08.279870+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_38cb0cb1b4eb38a7207c7a9a610266f3979bcfafb65818732e55501ab8042104","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 17 of arXiv:2505.20799, 'A note on the improved sparse Hanson-Wright inequalities'] Let $\delta\in\mathbb{R}^d$ and $B\in \mathbb{R}^{d\times m}$  as in Theorem \ref{thm:RIP}. For a fixed vector $x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$, denote $A=xx^\T$. For any $r\ge 1$, we have
