{"assumptions":["α real; for the infinite-expansion statements α irrational"],"created_at":"2026-09-13T01:59:57.491799+00:00","domain":"number theory, continued fractions","id":"claim_4dd0d9ece9166d1812156f3b42eca840a060258da83827d8e9c85f130a953389","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"Every real α has a regular continued fraction expansion α = a_0 + 1/(a_1 + 1/(a_2 + ...)) = [a_0; a_1, a_2, ...], produced by the Gauss algorithm: a_0 = floor(α), α_1 = 1/(α − a_0), a_k = floor(α_k), α_{k+1} = 1/(α_k − a_k). Here a_0 is an integer, every a_k (k ≥ 1) is an integer ≥ 1 (the partial quotients), and the α_k are the complete quotients, so a_k < α_k < a_k + 1 whenever the expansion continues. The expansion is finite iff α is rational (then it is unique once the last partial quotient is required to be ≥ 2); for irrational α it is infinite and unique.

The convergents p_n/q_n = [a_0; a_1, ..., a_n] are computed by p_{-1} = 1, q_{-1} = 0, p_0 = a_0, q_0 = 1 and p_{n+1} = a_{n+1} p_n + p_{n-1}, q_{n+1} = a_{n+1} q_n + q_{n-1} for n ≥ 0.

Basic identities: p_n q_{n-1} − p_{n-1} q_n = (−1)^{n-1} for n ≥ 0, so gcd(p_n, q_n) = 1; α = (p_n α_{n+1} + p_{n-1})/(q_n α_{n+1} + q_{n-1}); the even convergents increase strictly to α and the odd convergents decrease strictly to α (for irrational α); |α − p_n/q_n| < 1/(q_n q_{n+1}) < 1/q_n^2. The denominators satisfy 1 = q_0 ≤ q_1 < q_2 < q_3 < ..., with q_0 = q_1 exactly when a_1 = 1. The Gauss map x ↦ {1/x} on (0,1) preserves the Gauss measure dx/((1 + x) log 2), the ergodic-theoretic setting for the statistics of partial quotients.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":["$F:\mathbb{R}^{+}\to\mathbb{R}^{+}$ convex and nondecreasing","$A=(a_{ij})$ diagonal-free","$\xi_1,\dots,\xi_n$ independent, $(\tilde\xi_i)$ an independent copy of $(\xi_i)$"],"created_at":"2026-10-12T16:00:40+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4ed298efb7395ec4f31e577779851f026061749c9cf1f772e5afc0a879ba1bbb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 11 (Theorem 3.1.1 in \cite{de_la_Pena_Book}) of arXiv:2505.20799, 'A note on the improved sparse Hanson-Wright inequalities'] Let $F: \mathbb{R}^{+}\to \mathbb{R}^{+}$ be a convex nondecreasing function and $A=(a_{ij})_{n\times n}$ be a diagonal-free matrix. If $\{\xi_{i}, i\le n\}$ is a sequence of independent random variables and $\tilde{\xi}_{i}$ is an independent copy of $\xi_{i}$, then there exists a universal constant $C$ such that
