{"assumptions":["(S, d) is a metric space with its Borel sigma-algebra","mu_n and mu are Borel probability measures (equal total mass 1 is used in passing between closed-set and open-set forms)","test functions in (a),(b) are bounded; boundary-null condition in (e) is with respect to the limit measure mu"],"created_at":"2026-09-13T02:01:13.770491+00:00","domain":"probability, weak convergence of measures","id":"claim_d0baadf4b29d748af848cbc113b20c8c4f86d430309fbb655a5f9b9a1ab98e71","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"Let $(S, d)$ be a metric space with its Borel $\sigma$-algebra, and let $\mu_n$ ($n \ge 1$) and $\mu$ be Borel PROBABILITY measures on $S$. The following are equivalent:
  (a) $\mu_n \Rightarrow \mu$, i.e. $\int f \, d\mu_n \to \int f \, d\mu$ for every bounded continuous $f: S \to \mathbb{R}$;
  (b) $\int f \, d\mu_n \to \int f \, d\mu$ for every bounded uniformly continuous $f$ (it suffices to take bounded Lipschitz $f$);
  (c) $\limsup_n \mu_n(F) \le \mu(F)$ for every closed set $F \subset S$;
  (d) $\liminf_n \mu_n(G) \ge \mu(G)$ for every open set $G \subset S$;
  (e) $\mu_n(A) \to \mu(A)$ for every Borel set $A$ with $\mu(\partial A) = 0$ (a $\mu$-continuity set), where $\partial A = \overline{A} \setminus \mathrm{int}(A)$;
  (f) $\int f \, d\mu_n \to \int f \, d\mu$ for every bounded Borel function $f$ whose set of discontinuity points $D_f$ satisfies $\mu(D_f) = 0$.
The same equivalences hold on $S = \mathbb{R}$ with (e) restricted to half-lines: $\mu_n \Rightarrow \mu$ iff the distribution functions converge, $F_n(x) \to F(x)$, at every continuity point $x$ of $F$.

Sharp edges. (1) The equal total mass (probability measures, or at least finite measures with $\mu_n(S) \to \mu(S)$) is essential: for finite measures with $\mu_n(S) \not\to \mu(S)$, (c) alone or (d) alone does not give (a); e.g. $\mu_n = 0$ satisfies (c) against any $\mu$. Passing between (c) and (d) uses complements together with $\mu_n(S) = \mu(S) = 1$. (2) In (e) the boundary condition is on the LIMIT measure $\mu$, not on the $\mu_n$: $\delta_{1/n} \Rightarrow \delta_0$ but $\delta_{1/n}((0, \infty)) = 1 \not\to 0 = \delta_0((0,\infty))$ because $\delta_0(\partial (0,\infty)) = \delta_0(\{0\}) = 1$. (3) Bounded test functions only: $\mu_n = (1-1/n)\delta_0 + (1/n)\delta_n \Rightarrow \delta_0$ on $\mathbb{R}$ yet $\int x \, d\mu_n = 1 \not\to 0$; nothing is asserted about moments without uniform integrability. (4) The inequalities in (c) and (d) can be strict: $\delta_{1/n} \Rightarrow \delta_0$ with $F = \{0\}$ gives $\limsup \mu_n(F) = 0 < 1 = \mu(F)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-09-07T12:18:08.279009+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_da7898b5d45c53f7dad4798ef7383b01ef1cbb755a5cdaa8dc0f67b6c57ac3a7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 of arXiv:2505.20799, 'A note on the improved sparse Hanson-Wright inequalities'] Assume that $\{\delta_{i}, 1\le i\le n \}$ and $\{\zeta_{i}, 1\le i\le n \}$ are two independent sequences of random variables, where $\delta_{i}\sim\textnormal{Bernoulli}(p)$ are independent and $\zeta_{i}$ are independent $\alpha$-sub-exponential random variables with mean $0$ and variance $1$.
    Consider a random vector $\xi=(\xi_{1}, \cdots, \xi_{n})$  with $\xi_{i}=\delta_{i}\cdot\zeta_{i}$. Let $A$ be an $m\times n$ fixed matrix. Then for $0<\alpha\le 2$ and $t\ge 0$,
    \begin{align}
        \mathbb{P}\Big\{ \big\vert \Vert A\xi\Vert_{2}-\sqrt{p}\Vert A\Vert_{F} \big\vert>L^{2}(\alpha)t \Big\}\le 2\exp\Big\{-c(\alpha)\min \big\{\big(\frac{t}{\Vert A\Vert_{l_2\to l_2}}\big)^{2}, \big(\frac{t}{\Vert A\Vert_{l_2\to l_2}} \big)^{\alpha} \big\}    \Big\},
    \end{align}
    where $L(\alpha)=\max_{i}\Vert \zeta_i\Vert_{\Psi_{\alpha}}$ and $c(\alpha)>0$ is a constant depending only on $\alpha$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

