{"assumptions":["$\xi_1,\dots,\xi_n$ i.i.d. symmetric Weibull with scale $1$ and shape $\alpha\in[1,2]$: $\mathbb{P}(|\xi_i|>x)=e^{-x^{\alpha}}$, $x\ge 0$","$(\tilde\xi_j)$ an independent copy of $(\xi_i)$","$A=(a_{ij})$ a fixed symmetric $n\times n$ matrix with zero diagonal","$r\ge 2$"],"created_at":"2026-10-12T16:00:40+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_efd9ae72b56b5a1d0cdedd41fc0ff4751bbb25be87a5902abc10c0847f343d73","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 9 (Theorem 6.1 in \cite{Adamczak_PTRF}) of arXiv:2505.20799, 'A note on the improved sparse Hanson-Wright inequalities'] Let $\xi_1, \dots, \xi_n$ be i.i.d. symmetric Weibull variables with scale $1$ and shape parameter $\alpha$, that is $\mathbb{P}(|\xi_i| > x) = e^{-x^{\alpha}}$ for $x \ge 0$, let $(\tilde\xi_j)$ be an independent copy of $(\xi_i)$, and let $A = (a_{ij})$ be a fixed symmetric $n \times n$ matrix with zero diagonal; $\alpha^{*} = \alpha/(\alpha - 1)$ is the conjugate exponent ($\alpha^{*} = \infty$ when $\alpha = 1$, the norms below read as limits). In the case $1\le \alpha\le 2$, we have for $r\ge 2$
\begin{align}
		\Big\Vert \sum_{i,j}a_{ij}\xi_{i}\tilde{\xi}_{j}\Big\Vert_{L_{r}}\asymp_{\alpha}&r^{1/2}\Vert A\Vert_{F}+r\Vert A\Vert_{l_{2}\to l_{2}}+r^{1/\alpha}\Vert A\Vert_{l_{\alpha^{*}}(l_{2})}\nonumber\\
		&+r^{(\alpha+2)/2\alpha}\Vert A\Vert_{l_{2}\to l_{\alpha^{*}}}+r^{2/\alpha}\Vert A\Vert_{l_{\alpha}\to l_{\alpha^{*}}},\nonumber
	\end{align}
    where $\Vert A\Vert_{F}$ is the Frobenius norm, $\Vert A\Vert_{l_{p}(l_{2})} = \big(\sum_{i}(\sum_{j}a_{ij}^{2})^{p/2}\big)^{1/p}$, $\Vert A\Vert_{l_{p}\to l_{q}} = \sup\{\sum_{i,j}a_{ij}x_{j}y_{i} : \Vert x\Vert_{p}\le 1, \Vert y\Vert_{q^{*}}\le 1\}$ is the operator norm from $l_{p}$ to $l_{q}$ ($q^{*}$ the conjugate of $q$), and $\asymp_{\alpha}$ means that each side is at most a constant depending only on $\alpha$ times the other. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-09-07T12:18:08.279547+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f16b53ded540a28a3d8f5c61bef03affa594636b95fd7c8545fbb167fca40d3e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 13 (Corollary 4.10 in \cite{Ledoux_Book_concentration_measure}) of arXiv:2505.20799, 'A note on the improved sparse Hanson-Wright inequalities'] Let $\xi_{1}, \cdots, \xi_{n}$ be independent random variables such that $\vert \xi_{i}\vert\le K$ for all $1\le i\le n$. Assume that $f: \mathbb{R}^{n}\to \mathbb{R}$ is a convex and $1$-Lipschitz function. Then for $t>0$
