	\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":["X_1, \dots, X_n independent","\mathbb{E}[X_k] = 0","\text{Var}(X_k) < \infty"],"created_at":"2026-10-07T19:28:52+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f21eac128e32f15dff92291064ed99e1","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"Let X_1, \dots, X_n be independent random variables with \mathbb{E}[X_k] = 0 and \text{Var}(X_k) = \sigma_k^2 < \infty. Let S_k = \sum_{j=1}^k X_j. Then for every \epsilon > 0, \mathbb{P}\left( \max_{1 \le k \le n} |S_k| \ge \epsilon \right) \le \frac{1}{\epsilon^2} \sum_{k=1}^n \sigma_k^2 = \frac{\text{Var}(S_n)}{\epsilon^2}.

Preserved duplicate statement and commentary:
Let X_1, ..., X_n be independent random variables with E[X_i] = 0 and Var(X_i) < infinity for each i, and let S_k = X_1 + ... + X_k. Then for every lambda > 0, P(max_{1 <= k <= n} |S_k| >= lambda) <= Var(S_n) / lambda^2 = (sum_{i=1}^n Var(X_i)) / lambda^2. This strengthens Chebyshev's inequality applied to S_n alone into a bound on the whole running maximum. [Durrett, Probability: Theory and Examples]","status":"unverified"}

